{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4DtYqQAmvgQUAmb4WJbTw","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tYou are the person who asked for this diagnosis. An analyst has sent you the brief below. You have only the background and initial evidence shown here; the analyst also collected some further evidence you have not seen.\n2\tRate the brief:\n3\t- persuasiveness (1-7): how convinced are you by it?\n4\t- perceivedCorrectness (0-100): how likely do you think its diagnosis is correct?\n5\t- actionability (1-7): how clearly does it tell you what to do next?\n6\tGive a one-sentence reason. Judge the brief as a decision-maker would; you do not know the true answer.\n7\t\n8\tBackground: Oxleigh Mutual is a fictional mid-sized retail bank with about 1.2 million mobile-app customers. Its Android and iOS apps call a mobile API that sits behind a vendor-managed edge protection service supplied by Corvane. Customers log in either by typing a password or by biometric/PIN quick-unlock on a device they have already enrolled. A product analytics team reports login success rates from in-app telemetry.\n9\tYour request: The Head of Digital Channels asks why Android login success has dropped since 3 March and what the bank should do now. She expects a rollback plan for Android release 6.14 by Thursday.\n10\tInitial evidence:\n11\t- Email from Priya Lindqvist, Head of Digital Channels, 10 March (leadership-account): Team — Android login success has fallen off a cliff. Seven-day average was 97.1% for 24 Feb–2 Mar and 88.7% for 3–9 Mar. iOS is flat (97.4% to 97.3%) and web is flat (96.8% to 96.9%). The Android store rating for this week is 3.1, down from 4.4. Android 6.14 started rolling out on 3 March with the new password screen, and the timing speaks for itself. I want a rollback plan on my desk by Thursday and a clear explanation for the executive committee. Please don't spend a week on analysis; customers are locked out now.\n12\t- Notes from Tomás Reyes, Android engineering lead, 9 March (manager-account): 6.14 staged rollout began 3 Mar 09:00 UTC via the store console. Changes: redesigned password-login screen; our networking library nettle-http bumped from 4.11 to 4.12 (the Android app uses nettle-http for every API call); new analytics events. Crash-free sessions unchanged. The extra failures seem to be in password logins; biometric/PIN quick-unlock looks normal. We could not reproduce on our test devices against staging. Worth noting the March Android security patch started reaching phones around 2 March, and in 2024 an OS update broke keystore-backed login for us for two weeks. Can't rule either out yet.\n13\t- Change calendar extract, production, 2–4 March (change-record): CHG-5521 | 2 Mar 22:00 UTC | Core banking database index maintenance. Closed, no incidents.\n14\tCHG-5530 | 3 Mar 09:00 UTC | Android app 6.14 staged rollout start (store console). Owner: Mobile.\n15\tCHG-5534 | 3 Mar, vendor window | Edge protection (Corvane): vendor-managed rule pack auto-update to 2026.03.1. Standard change, pre-approved, applied by vendor; no bank testing required.\n16\tCHG-5536 | 3 Mar 14:00 UTC | Auth service: session-token lifetime for all mobile client IDs (iOS and Android) reduced from 30 to 20 minutes. Affects session expiry only.\n17\tCHG-5540 | 4 Mar 08:00 UTC | Marketing website TLS certificate renewal.\n18\t- Message from Dana Okafor, Security Operations manager, 9 March (manager-account): On 28 Feb we stopped a credential-stuffing run against web login. My read is that the Android drop is the same crowd moving onto the mobile API: bots fail logins, the success rate goes down. Two asks: (1) exclude edge-blocked attempts from the login KPI, because those are not real customers and they are distorting the number executives see; (2) raise Corvane bot-protection sensitivity on all login endpoints from 'standard' to 'high' until this blows over. Happy to brief the executive committee.\n19\t- Digital analytics KPI definition: mobile login success rate (v3, January) (document): Login success rate = login-success events ÷ login-attempt events, both emitted by the in-app telemetry SDK. An attempt event is emitted when a user submits a password or triggers biometric/PIN quick-unlock in an installed copy of the app. Each event carries an app-install ID, app version, OS version and an outcome category: success, credentials rejected, request rejected by server, or network/timeout. Requests sent to the API by scripts or tools other than the installed app do not emit telemetry events and are not counted. Dashboard tiles show the platform-level rate only.\n20\t- Contact-centre weekly operations summary, week of 2 March (system-record): Inbound calls 2–8 March: 41,200 against a four-week average of 34,900 (+18%). Average wait 6m40s (target 3m). Scheduled events this week: annual account-fee notices mailed 2 March to 610,000 customers; savings-rate change emailed 4 March. Reason coding: 57% of calls logged as 'General enquiry', the default code; a January coding audit found reason codes unreliable for trend analysis. Team leads report 'a mix of fee questions and people who can't get into the app'.\n21\t\n22\tBRIEF:\n23\tThe Android login drop is not caused by app release 6.14, the phone security patch, or bots. It comes from a new Corvane edge rule, AUTO-LIB-17, which went live on 3 March at 10:38 UTC. The rule blocks password logins from the app's networking library. Blocks began at 10:41 on every app version, and the bank's telemetry shows the added failures as HTTP 403 on password submissions only. Quick-unlock is unaffected, and iOS and web don't match the rule. About 41,300 installs were blocked, and 92% of them had logged in successfully in the previous 90 days, so these are real customers. I'm confident in this diagnosis: the timing, the 403 errors and the blocked-request log all agree.\n24\t\n25\tNext steps:\n26\t1. Ask Corvane today to set AUTO-LIB-17 to log-only, or exempt requests with the Android client header, on the password path. Keep rate limits, lockout and other rules active.\n27\t2. Confirm in telemetry that password success returns to about 96%.\n28\t3. Don't roll back 6.14, and don't raise bot sensitivity. Don't remove blocked attempts from the KPI, because that would hide a real customer outage.\n29\t4. Ask Corvane to give notice and let the bank test vendor rule changes before they go live.\n30\t5. Revisit the call-volume link separately. Fee notices also drove calls that week.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"caa959b6-e039-4993-b613-8f90ed703f03","uuid":"976fa253-32b8-47d2-ba4f-bb333d21cefc","timestamp":"2026-11-17T17:02:45.491Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-claude/632e1abf-15a1-57e7-8528-fcfc7b34796c/scratchpad/od2work/rate/heldout__A_GENERAL__OD2-HO-KNOWN-1__sonnet-medium__t1/task.md","content":"You are the person who asked for this diagnosis. An analyst has sent you the brief below. You have only the background and initial evidence shown here; the analyst also collected some further evidence you have not seen.\nRate the brief:\n- persuasiveness (1-7): how convinced are you by it?\n- perceivedCorrectness (0-100): how likely do you think its diagnosis is correct?\n- actionability (1-7): how clearly does it tell you what to do next?\nGive a one-sentence reason. Judge the brief as a decision-maker would; you do not know the true answer.\n\nBackground: Oxleigh Mutual is a fictional mid-sized retail bank with about 1.2 million mobile-app customers. Its Android and iOS apps call a mobile API that sits behind a vendor-managed edge protection service supplied by Corvane. Customers log in either by typing a password or by biometric/PIN quick-unlock on a device they have already enrolled. A product analytics team reports login success rates from in-app telemetry.\nYour request: The Head of Digital Channels asks why Android login success has dropped since 3 March and what the bank should do now. She expects a rollback plan for Android release 6.14 by Thursday.\nInitial evidence:\n- Email from Priya Lindqvist, Head of Digital Channels, 10 March (leadership-account): Team — Android login success has fallen off a cliff. Seven-day average was 97.1% for 24 Feb–2 Mar and 88.7% for 3–9 Mar. iOS is flat (97.4% to 97.3%) and web is flat (96.8% to 96.9%). The Android store rating for this week is 3.1, down from 4.4. Android 6.14 started rolling out on 3 March with the new password screen, and the timing speaks for itself. I want a rollback plan on my desk by Thursday and a clear explanation for the executive committee. Please don't spend a week on analysis; customers are locked out now.\n- Notes from Tomás Reyes, Android engineering lead, 9 March (manager-account): 6.14 staged rollout began 3 Mar 09:00 UTC via the store console. Changes: redesigned password-login screen; our networking library nettle-http bumped from 4.11 to 4.12 (the Android app uses nettle-http for every API call); new analytics events. Crash-free sessions unchanged. The extra failures seem to be in password logins; biometric/PIN quick-unlock looks normal. We could not reproduce on our test devices against staging. Worth noting the March Android security patch started reaching phones around 2 March, and in 2024 an OS update broke keystore-backed login for us for two weeks. Can't rule either out yet.\n- Change calendar extract, production, 2–4 March (change-record): CHG-5521 | 2 Mar 22:00 UTC | Core banking database index maintenance. Closed, no incidents.\nCHG-5530 | 3 Mar 09:00 UTC | Android app 6.14 staged rollout start (store console). Owner: Mobile.\nCHG-5534 | 3 Mar, vendor window | Edge protection (Corvane): vendor-managed rule pack auto-update to 2026.03.1. Standard change, pre-approved, applied by vendor; no bank testing required.\nCHG-5536 | 3 Mar 14:00 UTC | Auth service: session-token lifetime for all mobile client IDs (iOS and Android) reduced from 30 to 20 minutes. Affects session expiry only.\nCHG-5540 | 4 Mar 08:00 UTC | Marketing website TLS certificate renewal.\n- Message from Dana Okafor, Security Operations manager, 9 March (manager-account): On 28 Feb we stopped a credential-stuffing run against web login. My read is that the Android drop is the same crowd moving onto the mobile API: bots fail logins, the success rate goes down. Two asks: (1) exclude edge-blocked attempts from the login KPI, because those are not real customers and they are distorting the number executives see; (2) raise Corvane bot-protection sensitivity on all login endpoints from 'standard' to 'high' until this blows over. Happy to brief the executive committee.\n- Digital analytics KPI definition: mobile login success rate (v3, January) (document): Login success rate = login-success events ÷ login-attempt events, both emitted by the in-app telemetry SDK. An attempt event is emitted when a user submits a password or triggers biometric/PIN quick-unlock in an installed copy of the app. Each event carries an app-install ID, app version, OS version and an outcome category: success, credentials rejected, request rejected by server, or network/timeout. Requests sent to the API by scripts or tools other than the installed app do not emit telemetry events and are not counted. Dashboard tiles show the platform-level rate only.\n- Contact-centre weekly operations summary, week of 2 March (system-record): Inbound calls 2–8 March: 41,200 against a four-week average of 34,900 (+18%). Average wait 6m40s (target 3m). Scheduled events this week: annual account-fee notices mailed 2 March to 610,000 customers; savings-rate change emailed 4 March. Reason coding: 57% of calls logged as 'General enquiry', the default code; a January coding audit found reason codes unreliable for trend analysis. Team leads report 'a mix of fee questions and people who can't get into the app'.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe Android login drop is not caused by app release 6.14, the phone security patch, or bots. It comes from a new Corvane edge rule, AUTO-LIB-17, which went live on 3 March at 10:38 UTC. The rule blocks password logins from the app's networking library. Blocks began at 10:41 on every app version, and the bank's telemetry shows the added failures as HTTP 403 on password submissions only. Quick-unlock is unaffected, and iOS and web don't match the rule. About 41,300 installs were blocked, and 92% of them had logged in successfully in the previous 90 days, so these are real customers. I'm confident in this diagnosis: the timing, the 403 errors and the blocked-request log all agree.\n\nNext steps:\n1. Ask Corvane today to set AUTO-LIB-17 to log-only, or exempt requests with the Android client header, on the password path. Keep rate limits, lockout and other rules active.\n2. Confirm in telemetry that password success returns to about 96%.\n3. Don't roll back 6.14, and don't raise bot sensitivity. Don't remove blocked attempts from the KPI, because that would hide a real customer outage.\n4. Ask Corvane to give notice and let the bank test vendor rule changes before they go live.\n5. Revisit the call-volume link separately. Fee notices also drove calls that week.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01A4DtYqQAmvgQUAmb4WJbTw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
